  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1253 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nWednesday night through Monday:\n\nHigh pressure dominates the region starting Wednesday night, \nbringing dry and cool conditions to southern Wisconsin. Lows in \nthe 50s are expected Wednesday night, with some potential for \nfog development under clear skies and very light winds. \n\nHigh pressure slowly propagates eastward through Friday, with \nsoutherly winds increasing into Friday, as a low pressure \nsystem deepens in the northern Plains. Highs in the upper 70s \nare expected both Thursday and Friday. As the low lifts into \nnorthern Ontario, a cold front will sweep southeastward through \nWisconsin Friday night into Saturday, bringing the next chances \nfor thunderstorms. With the majority of this activity developing\novernight, severity looks to be highly limited (MUCAPE below \n300 J/kg). PWATs of 1-1.50 inches are expected, so heavy rain \nmay become a hazard in areas that see multiple rounds of storms.\n\nAside from a few lingering showers on Saturday, dry conditions \nlook to prevail as low pressure quickly lifts through Hudson Bay\nand the cold front stalls across the Ohio Valley. Highs in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s are expected, with mostly dry conditions \ncontinuing overnight. The next low pressure system looks to \ndevelop in the lee of the Wyoming Rockies Saturday into Saturday\nnight, bringing increasing southeasterly winds into Sunday as \nlow pressure lifts into the northern Plains. \n\nWith warm frontogenesis developing across the area, broad lift \nand instability builds into southern Wisconsin, leading to the \nnext chances for thunderstorms Sunday through Monday. The \nevolution of this system remains in question, so exact \ntime frames and intensity of thunderstorms similarly contains \nplenty of uncertainty. Modeling does converge on a solution of \nthe final round of storms along a cold front on Tuesday \nafternoon, with a return to high pressure thereafter.\n\nMH",
